ragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"As for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"dijkstraShortestPath(from:cutoff:successors:success:)"},{"type":"text","text":": it bounds"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the distance from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"start"},{"type":"text","text":", not the distance plus the estimate (NetworkX’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"astar_path"},{"type":"text","text":" bounds"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the sum), so the answer does not depend on the heuristic."}]}],"name":"cutoff"}],"kind":"parameters"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"discussion","text":"Discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With an admissible heuristic (never more than the true remaining cost, and so zero or less at a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"goal) the path is a shortest one, even when the heuristic is not consistent, since a state whose"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"distance improves after it was expanded is expanded again. With a consistent one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"h(u) <= cost(u, v) + h(v)"},{"type":"text","text":") no state is expanded twice. A heuristic of zero is Dijkstra’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"algorithm."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"style":"note","type":"aside","name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"As for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"dijkstraShortestPath(from:cutoff:successors:success:)"},{"type":"text","text":"; no priority"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(a distance plus its state’s estimate) is NaN, so no estimate is NaN, and no estimate is an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"infinity opposite to an infinite distance."}]}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/shortestpaths\/astarshor